  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 462,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63663,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 231,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most arise in the fundus.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T16:55:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63664,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 231,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "<p>Clinical features include abnormal uterine bleeding and infertility, or endometrial polyp may be asymptomatic.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-08-12T14:49:50"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63665,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 231,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most affected women are perimenopausal or postmenopausal. Polyp is a common endometrial lesion in patients who are on tamoxifen therapy {10202670; 15297160}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T16:55:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63666,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 231,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T16:55:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63667,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 231,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The stromal component is clonal {1384681}. There are rearrangements of one of the high mobility group protein genes&nbsp;&ndash; <em>HMGA1</em> (6p21-p22) or <em>HMGA2</em> (12q13-q15)&nbsp;&ndash; or of the 7q22 region {7882366}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T16:55:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63668,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 231,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>There is a narrow or broad-based stalk, with a fibrotic cut surface. Tamoxifen-related polyps are large and often multiple.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T16:55:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63669,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 231,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "<p>Clusters of irregularly shaped glands with the long axis parallel to the surface alternate with thick-walled blood vessels and hypercellular, hypocellular, or fibrous stroma {15252313; 29445890}, which may (rarely) contain bizarre degenerated stromal cells. Glands are usually inactive and cystic, with tubal or ciliated metaplasia, but they may occasionally be functional. Torsion of polyp, which is common, results in haemorrhagic infarction, epithelial metaplasia, and reactive atypia. In patients who are on hormones, there may be coexisting papillary proliferation {23211295}. Glands in tamoxifen-treated polyps are commonly staghorn-shaped, with a periglandular cuff of stromal cells, but unlike in adenosarcoma, the stromal cells are benign and mitotically inactive {25118811; 11914630}. When stromal smooth muscle is prominent, the polyp is considered adenomyomatous {23926192}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-07T12:37:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63670,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 231,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T16:55:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63671,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 231,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T16:55:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63672,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 231,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> branched and/or cystically dilated glands in altered stroma.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> there may be thick-walled blood vessels.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T16:55:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63673,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 231,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T16:55:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63674,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 231,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Although polyps are benign, concurrent/subsequent hyperplasia and carcinoma, respectively, occur in 11&ndash;30% and 0.5&ndash;3% of patients with endometrial polyps {19851198; 10746849; 17565613}. Polyps in postmenopausal women with abnormal vaginal bleeding are more likely to be malignant {18156974; 31009859}. The frequency of carcinoma reaches 10.7% in tamoxifen-related cases {15297160}. Involvement by serous carcinoma within a polyp may be subtle and may require immunohistochemistry for confirmation {30789501}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T16:55:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]